  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how a silicon nitride microresonator chip converts a single laser input into a broad optical frequency comb. A green laser beam is coupled into a straight silicon nitride channel on a silicon substrate, which feeds light into a circular ring resonator. Inside this ring, the light circulates and forms a localized pulse labeled as a soliton, shown together with a narrow spectral peak.\n\nThe main idea is that the resonator reshapes the incoming continuous laser light into many evenly spaced frequencies at the output. This is depicted by the rainbow-like beam leaving the chip and the accompanying spectrum made of multiple discrete lines, indicating a comb of frequencies rather than one single color. The contrast between the simple input spectrum and the multi-line output spectrum highlights the conversion process.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes the device structure—waveguide, ring resonator, and substrate—and the physical mechanism: soliton generation in a silicon nitride microresonator produces a stable frequency comb on an integrated photonic chip.",
